  <div class="lh-container">

<header class="main-header">
  <div class="series-title">Likutay Halachos · Choshen Mishpat</div>
  <h1>Hilchos G'viyas Milveh 2</h1>
  <div class="subtitle">Laws of Debt Collection — 2 (Continuous essay, abbreviated form)</div>
</header>
<div class="halachah-intro">
The matter of returning the pledge. A daytime garment — during the day. A nighttime garment — at night. And the matter of the prohibition against entering the borrower's house to seize his pledge.
</div>
<div class="verse-block">
  <span class="scripture">Sukkah = da'as = joining day and night — tz'dakah raises the poor from "animal" to "man"</span>
  <span class="source-ref">Based on Likutay Moharan I:266 "V'Yaakov Nasa Sukkosah"</span>
</div>
<p>Based on the teaching on the verse <span class="scripture">"And Yaakov traveled to Sukkos, and he built [a house]... and for his livestock he made booths"</span> <span class="source">(Genesis 33:17)</span>, Siman 266; see there. Through the mitzvah of <span class="key-term">sukkah</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">da'as</span> [knowledge/awareness] — through this one ascends from [the level of] <span class="key-term">b'heimah</span> [animal] to <span class="key-term">adam</span> [man].</p>
<p>And this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">tz'dakah</span> and <span class="key-term">halva'as chain</span> [a gracious loan] — through which one raises the poor man from <span class="key-term">b'heimah</span> to <span class="key-term">adam</span>. For <span class="scripture">"the wisdom of the poor man is despised"</span> <span class="source">(Ecclesiastes 9:16)</span>. And therefore when he is poor, his wisdom is blemished. And therefore he is called the aspect of <span class="key-term">b'heimah</span>, which is the absence of <span class="key-term">da'as</span>. As Dovid said <span class="source">(Psalms 73:22)</span>: <span class="scripture">"And I was brutish and did not know; I was as an animal before You."</span> For Dovid would always call himself poor and destitute, for he was constantly engaged in his prayer to rectify this attribute [of <span class="key-term">malchus</span>], as is known.</p>
<p>And this is what Rabbeinu, <em>zichrono livrachah</em>, wrote (Siman 37): that through <span class="key-term">tz'dakah</span> one enters the category of <span class="key-term">adam</span>. For this is measure for measure: through raising the poor man from <span class="key-term">b'heimah</span> to <span class="key-term">adam</span>, therefore he too merits to ascend to the category of <span class="key-term">adam</span> through this.</p>
<p>And the <span class="key-term">da'as</span> is the aspect of <span class="key-term">sukkah</span>, as above. And the <span class="key-term">sukkah</span> applies during the day and at night. For the <span class="key-term">sukkah</span> is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">ananai kavod</span> [clouds of glory], regarding which it is written: <span class="scripture">"The pillar of cloud did not depart by day, nor the pillar of fire by night"</span> <span class="source">(Exodus 13:22)</span> etc. For the essential <span class="key-term">da'as</span> is the aspect of the joining of <span class="key-term">chesed</span> and <span class="key-term">g'vurah</span>, which are the aspects of day and night. And <span class="key-term">sukkah</span> is the aspect of <span class="key-term">da'as</span>, and therefore the pillar of cloud completes etc. For the essential <span class="key-term">da'as</span> — which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">sukkah</span>, the aspect of the <span class="key-term">ananai kavod</span> — is the aspect of the joining of day and night, as above.</p>
<p>And therefore one must return the pledge during the day and at night — that is, a daytime garment during the day etc. — in order to complete and join day and night, in order to rectify the <span class="key-term">da'as</span> of the poor man. For the essential rectification of poverty is through joining day and night, as is known — which is the aspect of the completeness of the <span class="key-term">da'as</span>, as above. For holy <span class="key-term">mamon</span> [wealth] is the completeness of the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span>. For the <span class="key-term">nefesh</span> has its root in <span class="key-term">mamon</span>. And one must exert oneself with the poor man to complete his <span class="key-term">nefesh</span> and his <span class="key-term">da'as</span>, so that he will go from poverty to wealth, as it is written: <span class="scripture">"And you shall support him"</span> <span class="source">(Leviticus 25:35)</span> — through a loan, as it is written there: <span class="scripture">"Do not take from him interest"</span> <span class="source">(Leviticus 25:36)</span> etc. For one must take hold of his hand etc.</p>
<div class="editorial-note">
All of this was not written in its complete form, only in abbreviated chapter headings, for it was not written at its [proper] time and most of it was forgotten.
</div>

<div class="translator-summary">
<h3>Summary of Hilchos G'viyas Milveh 2</h3>
<p>A brief, intentionally abbreviated piece — Reb Nosson himself notes at the end that it was "not written in its complete form, only in abbreviated chapter headings, for it was not written at its proper time and most of it was forgotten." Based on LM I:266 "V'Yaakov Nasa Sukkosah."</p>
<p>The core mapping: sukkah = da'as = the joining of chesed and g'vurah (= day and night). Poverty blemishes the poor man's wisdom (chochmas hamisken b'zuyah), reducing him to the level of b'heimah (absence of da'as). Tz'dakah and halva'as chain raise him back to adam. Returning the pledge by day and by night = completing/joining day and night = rectifying the poor man's da'as. Measure for measure: one who raises the poor man to adam himself ascends to adam.</p>
<p class="translator-note">Based on Likutay Moharan I:266 "V'Yaakov Nasa Sukkosah"</p>
</div>

  </div>
</Layout>